  "query_preview": "..\nThe next several days will continue to remain rather quiet\nweather wise, with upper level ridging and surface high pressure\nremaining fixed over the Northeast and Mid-Atlantic states. Low\nlevel easterly flow with a long fetch off the Atlantic will\ncontinue to bring in slightly higher dewpoints into the Piedmont\nthrough Thursday morning, which will again lead to some low\nclouds developing across the area. These clouds look to\ndissipate by the 9-10am timeframe across these locations. The\nincreased moisture may also lead to fog development across the\narea as well, with higher probabilities of this development\nexpected across mountain locations. Beyond tomorrow, the area\nlooks to remain dry, with minimal cloud cover expected through\nthe end of the work week. \n\nBy Halloween, upper level clouds look to push into the region\nfrom the next system that looks to move across the conus. This\nshould bring temperatures back down to near normal values after\nFriday's well above normal forecast high temperatures.\nRegardless, dry weather will continue through the end of the\nMonth, leading to a pleasant trick or treat experience for all\nacross the region! \n\nSome uncertainty has developed in the forecast with early next\nweeks potential rain maker. Both the GFS and ECMWF keep the\nregion predominantly dry through next Tuesday as high pressure\nto our north looks to maintain enough dry air across the region\nto prevent precipitation from falling. The ECMWF is the most\nbullish with this aggressive high pressure system and weaker\nshortwave across the deep south that the area completely stays\ndry through much of the next week. Current ensemble spread\nbetween both the GEFS and EPS have the area receiving less than\n0.25 inches of rainfall areawide from this early week system.\nExpect rain chances to remain low for this system for now as\nmodels come into better agreement. \n\nBy the end of next week, there appears to be decent ensemble\nsupport in the GEFS and EPS ensembles for an amplified upper \nlevel trough digging south into the Gulf states before lifting \nto the northeast through the region by the Thursday/Friday \ntimeframe. Timing and specifics on this system are still unknown\nat this time; however, widespread rainfall will certainly be on\ntap for the end of next week as this system approaches.",
